cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you refer to this work, please cite the repository. It is the audit
  trail for a dissertation, and its findings are negative: they are
  citable as measurements of what these methods achieved on this cohort
  at this label quality, and not as a method anyone should adopt.

title: >-
  cleft-aesthetics: automated aesthetic assessment of surgical outcome in
  cleft lip and palate repair, and a record of what could and could not
  be shown on a 237-patient cohort

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE FILE WHERE THE ANONYMISATION DOES NOT APPLY.
#
# No person is named anywhere else in the tracked set and a standing
# guard in the suite enforces that. A citation exists to name its author,
# so the guard PERMITS THIS FILE SPECIFICALLY rather than being widened:
# see CITATION_EXEMPT in tests/test_phase9.py, which also extends the
# sweep to the repository root and to .cff so that this permission is a
# decision rather than a gap.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
authors:
  - given-names: El Mahdi
    family-names: Liamani
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"

# The public snapshot's own URL. Each snapshot is one commit copying the
# tracked file state of a private development repository, so this is the
# artifact a reader can reach, not the history that produced it.
repository-code: "https://github.com/xRedRingx/cleft-aesthetics-pub"

abstract: >-
  A research codebase and its audit trail. The task is predicting a
  five-rater panel's aesthetic grade of surgical outcome from a clinical
  photograph, on a cohort of 237 patients. The headline result is
  negative and measured rather than assumed: the best arm reaches a
  Pearson correlation of 0.2520 against a panel mean whose own
  reliability caps any predictor in the range [0.8983, 0.9065], and no
  alternative feature source, training objective or label construction
  beat that baseline by an amount the cohort can resolve. The repository
  carries the pre-registrations, rulings and readings that were written
  before each result existed, an append-only claim ledger in which five
  of thirty-nine recorded comparisons are claimable, and the corrections
  the project made to its own figures, dated in place with the originals
  preserved. No patient data is present and none can be.

keywords:
  - cleft lip and palate
  - aesthetic outcome assessment
  - clinical machine learning
  - inter-rater reliability
  - negative results
  - reproducibility
  - pre-registration

type: software

license: Apache-2.0

# The licence covers this repository's own work. ONE FILE IS PUBLISHED ON
# A DIFFERENT BASIS: src/cleft/models/srgnn.py is a port of an upstream
# implementation that carries no LICENSE file, so nothing was inherited
# from it, and it appears here by permission held directly from the
# supervisor, who is an author on that paper, and from the owner of the
# upstream repository, who is its corresponding author. That permission
# grants nothing onward. The NOTICE file states this at the top level and
# cleft.models.srgnn.PUBLICATION_AUTHORISED is the dated record.
#
# CFF takes a single SPDX identifier, so the exception cannot live in
# this field. Naming Apache-2.0 here and the exception in NOTICE is the
# closest honest fit the format allows.
